'''3.''' Empty or unoccupied; void; bare.
{{smaller block|<poem>So blacken’d all her world in secret, ''blank''
And waste it seem’d and vain.</poem>
{{right|''Tennyson'', Princess, vii.}}
<poem>Now slowly falls the dull ''blank'' night.</poem>
{{right|''Bryant'', Rain-Dream.}}
Specifically — (''a'') Free from written or printed characters; not written upon: as, a ''blank'' book; ''blank'' paper; ''blank'' spaces. (''b'') Not filled up: applied to legal, banking, commercial, or other forms: as, a ''blank'' check or order; a ''blank'' ballot; a ''blank'' bond. (''c'') Of uniform surface; unrelieved or unbroken by ornament or opening: as, a ''blank'' wall. (''d'') Empty of results, of interest, etc.: as, a ''blank'' outlook for the future.}}
'''4.''' Without contents; especially, wanting some part necessary to completeness: as, ''blank'' cartridges, that is, cartridges containing powder but no ball.—'''5.''' Vacant in expression; exhibiting perplexity, real or feigned; nonplussed; disconcerted.
{{smaller block|<poem>Never be ''blank'', Alonzo,
Because this fellow has outstript thy fortune.</poem>
{{right|''Fletcher'', Rule a Wife, ii. 2.}}
The Damsell of Burgundie, at sight of her own letter, was soon ''blank'', and more ingenuous then to stand outfacing.
{{right|''Milton'', Eikonoklastes, xxi.}}}}
'''6.''' Complete; utter; unmitigated: as, “''blank'' stupidity,” ''Percival.''
{{smaller block|<poem>All but the suffering heart was dead
For him abandoned to ''blank'' awe,
To vacancy, and horror strong.</poem>
{{right|''Wordsworth'', White Doe of Rylstone, vi.}}}}
