'''blanket''' (blang′ket), ''v. t.'' [< ''blanket'', ''n.''] '''1.''' To cover with a blanket or as with a blanket: as, to ''blanket'' a horse.
{{smaller block|I’ll . . . ''blanket'' my loins.{{right|''Shak.'', Lear, ii. 3.}}
<poem>''Blanketted'' like a dog,
And like a cut-purse whipt.</poem>{{right|''Massinger'', Parliament of Love, iv. 5.}}
The importance of the ''blanketing'' action of our atmospheric constituents has been in no way over-stated.{{right|''Science'', V. 450.}}}}
'''2.''' To toss in a blanket by way of punishment or practical joke.
{{smaller block|We’ll have our men ''blanket'' ’em i’ the hall.{{right|''B. Jonson'', Epicœne, v. 4.}}}}
'''3.''' To take the wind out of the sails of, as the sails of one vessel when it is passing close to windward of another.
{{smaller block|B’s helmsman will be apt to sail his boat as close to the wind as possible, and try to “claw to windward,” and prevent A from ''blanketing'' him.{{right|''Qualtrough'', Boat Sailer’s Manual, p. 135.}}}}

'''blanket-bar''' (blang′ket-bär), ''n.'' An iron bar used to keep the blanket of a printing-press in place.

'''blanket-clause''' (blang′ket-klâz), ''n.'' A general or indefinite clause framed so as to provide for a number of contingencies.
{{smaller block|Suitable annual appropriations . . . require no ''blan-''}}

